"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][5]={"school_physics_en:M5-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M5-L1 – Electric charge and electrostatic forces<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Electric charge and circuits<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A rubbed plastic balloon can stick to a wall. How can this happen\nwhen the wall may be electrically neutral overall? The explanation\nbegins with electric charges and their distribution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Electric charge<\/strong> is a physical property of particles\nand objects. Its symbol is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand its SI unit is the <strong>coulomb<\/strong>\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nCharge can be positive\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>+<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nor negative\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>−<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nAn object is <strong>electrically neutral<\/strong> when its positive and\nnegative contributions balance overall. Neutral does not mean that it\ncontains no charged particles.<\/p>\n<p>Protons in atoms are positively charged; electrons are negatively\ncharged. When suitable materials are brought into contact and then\nseparated, electrons can be transferred, leaving the materials charged.\nRubbing may increase this transfer. <strong>Total charge is\nconserved<\/strong>: charge is moved, not created from nothing.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Like charges repel<\/strong>\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+/+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-/-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Unlike charges attract<\/strong>\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+/-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A charged object can also attract a neutral object: the distribution\nof charge inside the neutral object can shift\n(<strong>polarisation<\/strong>). Attraction alone therefore cannot\nidentify the sign of an unknown object’s charge.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> Two pairs of charges with\nforce arrows and a polarised neutral object.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_025_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Charged objects displaying repulsion, attraction, and polarisation of a neutral object.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Charged objects displaying repulsion,\nattraction, and polarisation of a neutral object.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The diagram contrasts repulsion of like charges and attraction of\nunlike charges or polarised neutral objects.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>Two small spheres with the same negative net charge hang freely close\nto one another. They move apart, consistent with the charge model. If a\nnegatively charged plastic rod approaches a light paper ball that was\ninitially neutral, the ball may be attracted. This does\n<strong>not<\/strong> prove the paper has a positive net charge:\npolarisation can explain the observation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe classroom observation:<\/strong> A teacher rubs a plastic\nballoon with dry fabric and brings it near small paper pieces. Observe\nbefore contact and again after a brief touch. Keep the materials away\nfrom sensitive electronics. Do not use high-voltage devices, mains\noutlets or ignition sources. Record whether the observation establishes\na charge sign.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does “neutral” mean in the charge model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens between two positively charged spheres?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does attraction of neutral paper not prove an opposite net\ncharge?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A sphere loses electrons. How can that change its net charge?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The positive and negative contributions balance; charged particles\nare still present.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">They repel one another.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The charged object can redistribute charges inside the paper,\ncausing attraction.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Its charge becomes more positive relative to its initial state\nbecause it has lost negative charge.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The arrows indicate force directions, not magnitudes. The force also\ndepends on separation and amount of charge. Charging by friction depends\non materials and humidity. Electric field-line models will be developed\nlater.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics curriculum: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 3.5\nElectric current and charge · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/einfache-stromkreise/grundwissen/elektrizitaet-und-ladung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Electricity and charge<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Signs of charge, charge balancing,\nelectrostatic attraction and repulsion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M5-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M5-L2 – Electric current and the closed circuit<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Electric charge and circuits<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A battery, a small lamp, a switch and wires will only light the lamp\nif the circuit forms a complete path. A single wire from the battery to\nthe lamp is not enough.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>electric current<\/strong> is a directed transport of\nelectric charge. In metals, mobile electrons are the main charge\ncarriers; in other materials ions can also transport charge. A simple\ncircuit needs an energy source, conducting connections and a load (such\nas a suitable lamp).<\/p>\n<p>With a <strong>closed switch<\/strong> a complete conducting path\nexists; an <strong>open switch<\/strong> breaks the path. In the external\ncircuit, <strong>conventional current direction<\/strong> is defined from\nthe positive to the negative terminal of the source. In metallic wires\nthe mean electron drift is in the opposite direction. Electric signals\npropagate much faster than the electrons drift.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Current<\/strong> is the amount of charge transferred per unit\ntime:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t}, \\qquad [I]=\\mathrm{A},\\quad 1\\,\\mathrm{A}=1\\,\\mathrm{C/s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The source supplies energy; charge is not “used up” in the idealised\nclosed circuit. <strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> Battery,\nswitch and lamp in a closed circuit, with conventional current direction\nmarked.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_026_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Battery, closed switch and lamp connected in a continuous circuit with current-direction arrows.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Battery, closed switch and lamp connected\nin a continuous circuit with current-direction arrows.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The simple circuit provides a complete path and shows\nconventional current direction.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A wire carries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts average current is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\frac{8\\,\\mathrm{C}}{20\\,\\mathrm{s}}=0.40\\,\\mathrm{A}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At a constant current of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.15\\,\\mathrm{A}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe charge carried in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.0\\,\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Use only an approved current-limited classroom supply or a\nsuitable battery holder:<\/strong> A teacher or supervised learners\nconnect an appropriate low-voltage lamp through a switch. Assemble the\ncircuit with the source switched off; observe the lamp as the switch\nchanges position. Never short-circuit battery terminals or use a\nhousehold mains outlet.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What condition is necessary for the lamp to shine continuously?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A current transfers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which way do electrons drift in a metal compared with conventional\ncurrent?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is electric charge consumed in the lamp?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A complete conducting path and a suitable source and lamp are\nneeded.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=3/5\\,\\mathrm{A}=0.60\\,\\mathrm{A}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the opposite direction to conventional current.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Charge continues through the idealised steady circuit; energy is\nconverted.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The equation gives average current over a finite interval; changing\ncurrent requires a suitably short interval. A closed path does not\nguarantee light if the source or lamp is unsuitable. A short circuit is\nalso a closed path but is hazardous and not a working lamp circuit.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics curriculum: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 3.5\nElectric current and charge · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/elektrische-grundgroessen/grundwissen/elektrische-stromkreise-und-ihre-groessen\">LEIFIphysik\n– Electric circuits and their quantities<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Charge carriers, current\nand the concept of voltage · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/elektrische-grundgroessen/grundwissen/elektrische-stromstaerke-und-die-einheit-ampere\">LEIFIphysik\n– Electric current and the ampere<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Current as charge per unit time\nand its SI unit · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M5-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M5-L3 – Measuring voltage and current correctly<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Electric charge and circuits<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Two instruments might display “0.20 A” and “3.0 V”. They do not\nmeasure the same quantity and therefore must not be connected to the\ncircuit in the same way.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\nexpresses charge crossing a conductor section per unit time (unit:\nampere). <strong>Voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\nbetween two points expresses transferable energy per unit charge (unit:\nvolt). For an energy transfer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nassociated with a charge magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan appropriate idealised relation is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=\\frac{\\Delta E}{\\Delta Q},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{V}=1\\,\\mathrm{J/C}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>An <strong>ammeter (A)<\/strong> is connected <strong>in\nseries<\/strong> in the branch whose current is to be measured; current\npasses through it. A <strong>voltmeter (V)<\/strong> is connected\n<strong>in parallel<\/strong> across a component, between its two\nterminals. It compares the electrical potentials at its ends.<\/p>\n<p>An ideal ammeter has negligible internal resistance; an ideal\nvoltmeter has extremely high internal resistance. Meter range, polarity\nand socket selection must match the instrument and supply.\n<strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> A simple circuit with the\nammeter in series and a voltmeter parallel to the lamp.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_027_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"An ammeter in series and a voltmeter across a lamp in an extra-low-voltage circuit.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">An ammeter in series and a voltmeter\nacross a lamp in an extra-low-voltage circuit.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Current is measured in series; voltage is measured across two\ncomponent terminals.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>Measurements for a small lamp are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=3.0\\,\\mathrm{V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0.20\\,\\mathrm{A}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncharge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta Q=I\\Delta t=2.0\\,\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npasses a section of the circuit. If the readings stay approximately\nconstant, the electrical energy transferred at the lamp in that time\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E=U\\Delta Q=3.0\\,\\mathrm{V}\\cdot 2.0\\,\\mathrm{C}=6.0\\,\\mathrm{J}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p>Only on a <strong>current-limited extra-low-voltage circuit<\/strong>\nunder supervision: switch the supply off first. Select the meter\nterminals and ranges as instructed. Insert the ammeter into a break in\nthe branch and place the voltmeter across the two lamp terminals. Turn\nthe supply on only after teacher inspection. Never connect an ammeter\ndirectly across a battery; never experiment on mains voltage.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How is an ammeter connected to measure lamp current?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How is a voltmeter connected to measure voltage across that\nlamp?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate charge transferred in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.40\\,\\mathrm{A}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What energy corresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In series in the lamp branch; charge passes through the meter.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In parallel across the lamp terminals.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta Q=I\\Delta t=0.40\\,\\mathrm{A}\\cdot5\\,\\mathrm{s}=2.0\\,\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E=U\\Delta Q=2\\,\\mathrm{V}\\cdot5\\,\\mathrm{C}=10\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=\\Delta E/\\Delta Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequires a clear choice of the two measurement points and associated\nenergy transfer. Real instruments can slightly alter the circuit. The\nreadings apply only to the specified operating conditions; a lamp may\nchange resistance as it warms.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics curriculum: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 3.6\nCurrent, voltage, resistance and power · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/elektrische-grundgroessen/grundwissen/schaltung-von-messgeraeten\">LEIFIphysik\n– Connecting measuring instruments<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Ammeter in series, voltmeter in\nparallel · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/elektrische-grundgroessen/grundwissen/elektrische-stromkreise-und-ihre-groessen\">LEIFIphysik\n– Electric circuits and their quantities<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Charge carriers, current\nand the concept of voltage · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M5-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M5-L4 – Permanent magnets and the magnetic field<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Electric charge and circuits<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A compass turns when placed close to a magnet. The magnet can affect\nthe needle without touching it. A magnetic field describes this region\nof influence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>Every ordinary <strong>permanent magnet<\/strong> has a <strong>north\npole (N)<\/strong> and a <strong>south pole (S)<\/strong>. Like poles\nrepel and unlike poles attract. A piece of iron can be attracted by\neither pole; attraction alone does not show that the iron is an opposite\nmagnetic pole.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>magnetic field<\/strong> describes magnetic influence in\nspace. <strong>Field lines<\/strong> are drawn model curves, not physical\nthreads. Outside a bar magnet their conventional direction is from the\nnorth to the south pole; inside the magnet the closed path continues\nfrom south to north. A compass needle aligns with the local field\ndirection. Closely spaced model lines indicate a stronger field; the\nexact number drawn is arbitrary.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>Earth<\/strong> also has a magnetic field. A simple\nbar-magnet model approximately explains compass alignment. In reality\nthe field is generated mainly by electric currents in the fluid outer\ncore and varies in space and time. Geographic and magnetic poles are not\nidentical. <strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> Bar magnet,\nclosed field lines and a compass needle.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_028_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Bar magnet with north and south poles, external model field lines, and a compass needle.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Bar magnet with north and south poles,\nexternal model field lines, and a compass needle.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Field lines run from N to S outside the magnet and complete the\nloop within.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>Bring the north pole of one bar magnet towards the north pole of\nanother: they repel. Turn one around so that north faces south: the\npoles attract. A nearby compass may turn to a new direction; its\norientation depends on where it is placed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p>Place a small compass on a desk and slowly move a weak classroom\nmagnet around it at a safe distance. Sketch the needle orientation at\nseveral positions. Keep magnets away from medical implants, magnetic\nstorage and sensitive electronics. Avoid handling strong neodymium\nmagnets close to fingers (pinching risk).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which magnetic poles repel each other?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In which direction do conventional field lines run outside a bar\nmagnet?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Are field lines physical fibres?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why need a compass near a bar magnet not point towards geographic\nnorth?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two north poles or two south poles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">From N to S outside; the lines close through the magnet.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; they are a model for field direction and structure.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The nearby bar magnet changes the net local magnetic field.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>A bar-magnet model is not a full three-dimensional field description\nand only crudely approximates Earth’s geodynamo. Line count and\nthickness are drawing choices; a schematic alone gives no precise\nmagnetic-field magnitude.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics curriculum: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 3.9\nMagnetic fields and electromagnetic induction · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/permanentmagnetismus/grundwissen/magnetfeld-und-feldlinien\">LEIFIphysik\n– Magnetic field and field lines<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Field-line model, compass and\nEarth’s magnetic field · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/permanentmagnetismus/grundwissen/eigenschaften-von-permanentmagneten\">LEIFIphysik\n– Properties of permanent magnets<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Poles and attraction/repulsion ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M5-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M5-L5 – The magnetic effect of electric current<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Electric charge and circuits<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>An electromagnet can hold an object and release it again. This is\npossible because electric current creates a magnetic field that can be\nchanged by switching the circuit on and off.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>current-carrying conductor<\/strong> is surrounded by a\nmagnetic field. A <strong>coil<\/strong> combines the effects of its\nturns. With direct current, its two ends behave magnetically much like\nthe poles of a bar magnet. <strong>Reversing current direction<\/strong>\nswaps which end acts as north and which as south, provided the winding\norientation stays the same.<\/p>\n<p>The electromagnet’s effect depends on <strong>current<\/strong>,\n<strong>number of turns<\/strong> and <strong>core material<\/strong>.\nWith other conditions held equal, adding turns can strengthen the field.\nA suitable <strong>soft-iron core<\/strong> often increases the magnetic\neffect considerably. To test one variable, keep the others as constant\nas possible. Note that a coil with more turns may also have higher\nelectrical resistance, so current can change even when using the same\nbattery.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> Two comparison coils, a\ncurrent arrow and the variables held constant.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_029_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic comparison of coils with different turn counts and an optional soft-iron core.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic comparison of coils with\ndifferent turn counts and an optional soft-iron core.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>At controlled current, turn count and core material affect the\nmagnetic response.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>Compare coil A with 20 turns and coil B with 40 turns at the\n<strong>same current and similar geometry<\/strong>. The simple model\npredicts a stronger field for B. This does not imply that its lifting\nforce must double. Inserting a suitable soft-iron core into one coil\nwill usually increase the magnetic effect markedly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Teacher supervision required<\/strong>, using an approved\ncurrent-limited extra-low-voltage supply and a suitable classroom coil.\nWith power off, place a compass near the coil. Switch on briefly and\nobserve its deflection. Switch off again before moving the core or\nrebuilding the setup. Do not connect unprotected homemade wire coils\ndirectly to batteries: low resistance can cause overheating or a short\ncircuit. Do not use mains power or powerful magnets.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to coil poles when current direction is reversed?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must current be controlled when comparing coils with 20 and 40\nturns?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does a suitable soft-iron core usually do?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does twice the turn count guarantee twice the lifting force?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">North and south swap ends.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">More turns can change the resistance and thus current; the cause of\nany difference would otherwise be unclear.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It generally strengthens the coil’s magnetic effect.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Force also depends on field geometry, air gaps and\nmaterials.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>This is a qualitative school-level model. Field strength,\nmagnetisation and force are not always linear; iron cores may saturate.\nHeat generation limits how long a coil may safely remain on.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L6<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics curriculum: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 3.9\nMagnetic fields and electromagnetic induction · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/stromwirkungen/grundwissen/magnetische-wirkung-des-elektrischen-stroms\">LEIFIphysik\n– Magnetic effects of electric current<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Magnetic effect of a coil\nand a soft iron core · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M5-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M5-L6 – Electrical safety in learning and everyday life<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Electric charge and circuits<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A classroom experiment with a suitable small battery is not the same\nas an experiment involving a mains socket. Why are the safety rules\ndifferent, and why can even a battery become hot in a short circuit?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>The human body can conduct electric current. <strong>Current through\nthe body<\/strong> may cause serious injury; risk depends on current\npath, magnitude, duration and environmental conditions. Moisture can\nlower the electrical resistance of skin and surroundings. Household\n<strong>mains voltage must not be used for student\nexperiments<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>short circuit<\/strong> is an unintended very-low-resistance\nconnection, for example directly between two battery terminals. It can\ncause high current, heating or fire even if the supply voltage is\nmodest. Batteries and rechargeable cells are not necessarily safe\nagainst short circuits.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Precautions:<\/strong> Use only approved, suitable and\nsupervised classroom equipment; wire circuits with the power off; check\nthe supply and current limit; avoid damaged wires and moisture; never\nopen sockets or household electrical appliances; ask a teacher or\nqualified professional when unsure. Fuses and residual-current devices\ncan reduce risk but do not make touching live parts or improvised\nexperiments safe. <strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> Approved\nlow-voltage classroom arrangement contrasted with prohibited mains\nexperimentation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_030_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two panels comparing a suitable current-limited classroom circuit with a crossed-out mains outlet.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two panels comparing a suitable\ncurrent-limited classroom circuit with a crossed-out mains\noutlet.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Classroom experiments need a suitable current-limited supply;\ntesting mains electricity is prohibited.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>Situation A: A small lamp is wired to a suitable classroom supply\nthrough a switch while the supply is off. Under supervision, switching\nit on may be an appropriate teaching activity.<\/p>\n<p>Situation B: Someone tries to measure current at a mains socket by\ninserting the probes of a multimeter set to ammeter mode. This is a\n<strong>potentially lethal mistake<\/strong>: an ammeter has very low\ninternal resistance and may create a short circuit. The attempt\n<strong>must not be made<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>No mains experiment:<\/strong> Assess drawings only, or use an\napproved current-limited extra-low-voltage setup. On paper, mark (1) the\ncurrent limiter, (2) the switch, and (3) a point where directly joining\nthe positive and negative terminals would create a short circuit. Never\nmake that short circuit in reality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can moisture increase electrical danger?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must battery terminals not be joined directly by a wire?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does a residual-current device make it safe to touch live electrical\nconductors?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What should be done with a damaged mains cable?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Wet skin and surroundings can reduce resistance, making dangerous\ncurrent more likely.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Very low resistance can cause severe heating or damage.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. It is an additional protection device, not permission to contact\nlive parts.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Do not use or open it; report it to a responsible adult or qualified\nprofessional.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>This lesson is not professional electrical-safety training and does\nnot define any universally safe voltage limit. Safety depends on the\ncomplete setup and applicable school procedures. In a real electrical\naccident, protect yourself first, arrange a safe disconnection by\ncompetent persons and request emergency assistance; do not touch a\nperson who may still be in contact with live electrical equipment.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L1 – Position, distance, time and\nreference frames<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics curriculum: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 3.5\nElectric current and charge · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/elektrik\">Sichere\nSchule – Electrical experiments in physics lessons<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Supervision,\nsuitable voltage sources and safety precautions for school experiments ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/elektrische-grundgroessen/grundwissen/schaltung-von-messgeraeten\">LEIFIphysik\n– Connecting measuring instruments<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Ammeter in series, voltmeter in\nparallel · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
